      "hints": [
        "Integrate $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = 2x - 5$ to get $\\frac{dy}{dx} = x^2 - 5x + c$.",
        "Use $\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=3} = 0$ to find $c = 6$.",
        "Integrate again to get $y = \\frac{1}{3}x^3 - \\frac{5}{2}x^2 + 6x + d$ and use $(3, 6)$ to find $d = \\frac{3}{2}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Integrate the second derivative with respect to $x$:\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\int (2x - 5) dx = x^2 - 5x + c$$",
        "Since $(3, 6)$ is a stationary point, $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$ when $x = 3$:\n$$3^2 - 5(3) + c = 0 \\implies 9 - 15 + c = 0 \\implies c = 6$$\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx} = x^2 - 5x + 6$$",
        "Integrate the first derivative to find $y$:\n$$y = \\int (x^2 - 5x + 6) dx = \\frac{x^3}{3} - \\frac{5x^2}{2} + 6x + d$$",
        "Substitute the known coordinates $(3, 6)$ into the curve equation to determine $d$:\n$$6 = \\frac{3^3}{3} - \\frac{5(3^2)}{2} + 6(3) + d = 9 - 22.5 + 18 + d = 4.5 + d$$\n$$d = 6 - 4.5 = 1.5 = \\frac{3}{2}$$",
        "State the complete equation of the curve:\n$$y = \\frac{1}{3}x^3 - \\frac{5}{2}x^2 + 6x + \\frac{3}{2}$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Finding a Curve from Its Second Derivative": "Integrates to $x^2 - 5x + c$ (B1). Finds $c = 6$ (M1, A1). Integrates to $\\frac{x^3}{3} - \\frac{5x^2}{2} + 6x + d$ (B1 FT). Substitutes $(3, 6)$ to find $d = 3/2$ (M1). States curve equation (A1)."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
